A||||15||batboy||Help! Coil spring trouble!||||1
Z||000000||batboy||10-07-1999||09:26 PM||||I'm finally getting around to disassembling the front suspension of the Batmobile (85 Fiero) to prepare for some major upgrades. I have removed the sway bar and end links. The outer tie rods ends are off. The spindles have been removed from the ball joint studs. The coil spring is just hanging there, but appears to be stuck up at the top. I've pulled and tugged, got my biggest crowbar out and pried until I cussed, but can't get the spring out. It appears to be stuck tight at the top, hung up by a big rubber thingy. Am I missing something or is there some trick to getting this out? According to the Haynes manual, it should just drop out at this point, there is no tension on the spring at all. Help!<P>One other thing, my new shorter "sport" springs are suppose to lower the front end by about 1-1/2 inches, but the new springs appear to be almost 3 inches shorter than the originals and with one less coil (this is off the car of course). I know they are supposed to be stiffer (higher spring rate) which will compensate somewhat, but does this sound like it might be too short? Wait a minute, maybe there's no such thing as being too low in the front. Get down!||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||10-07-1999||09:56 PM||||you answered your own question. The lowered spring is a higher rate. Has to be or you'll bottom out all the time. Unless you like replacing lots of ball joints that's not a good thing to do. Lower Fiero Good. Broken Fiero Bad.<P>((Ogre's love seeing badly done low riders sitting in the middle of the road with blown front ends. Always gives a good laugh.))||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||10-07-1999||10:29 PM||||What about getting the old spring out? It appears to be stuck onto the rubber at the top of the coil spring. Shouldn't it just drop out?||||reg||1
Z||000003||RICK||10-07-1999||10:43 PM||||When I rebuilt my front end my springs did the same thing.  With a little twisting, pulling, shouting, and hiting they came right off.  The rubber on top has made a nice tight seal.   By the way, what springs are you using?||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||10-07-1999||11:45 PM||||The brand name is Suspension Techniques, these are what they call the sport lowering springs. I'll let you know how they work, if I ever get the old springs out and the new ones in, that is.||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||10-08-1999||01:40 AM||||I think the worst part is that the springs appear to be held up there by some sort of levitation after grunting everything else out.Some side to side rocking while pulling will work,eventually.<BR>When you get it going back together,and put your urethane swaybar bushings in,take a look at the bar,it should be horizontal,not pointing down from the forward mtg.to the control arm.You may need longer links to ensure full,proper action from the swaybar.<BR>Ogre,I like to be able and see the ball joint pieces,and the puzzled look on the driver's face in the same frame;very humorous indeed,usually the back side of a bicycle-sized tire also.<BR>That's my first suspension rule when friends ask me if their cars would work better lowered with springs,I measure the distance from the lower control arm pivot,then the lower balljoint pivot;the from the inside of the car,the arm should never point upwards while parked.Do it right,or not at all I say.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||10-08-1999||07:49 AM||||Ok, thanks for the info guys, I hope to complete the job this weekend. I tell you what, the inner tie rods were completely shot. I'm thinking of dropping the whole rack while I have everything apart and checking that out too. It almost looks like I have to anyway in order to remove one of the lower control arm bolts. Nothing's ever easy on these cars, huh.||||reg||1
Z||000007||peterh||10-08-1999||01:45 PM||||On my car one spring just dropped off when I didn't want too, the other one was stuck. I was not replacing springs, just cleaning and painting all front supension parts.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||10-08-1999||09:24 PM||||Ok, I finally got the springs out and the lower control arms off. Thanks for the advice about the springs, it took a combination of yanking back and forth and prying with a crowbar. The little ball joint remover tool I bought is too small to work on the Fiero lower ball joints, so I guess I'm taking the lower control arms to a shop tomorrow and having them pressed out and the new ones pressed in. I also get the pleasure of burning out the old bushings to prepare for the installation of the new Prothane poly bushings.||||reg||1
Z||000009||TOM||10-09-1999||12:02 AM||||Talk about too low. I once lowered a friends maxima 10 inches below stock, Thats low.||||reg||1
Z||000010||mshill||10-09-1999||01:32 AM||||batboy,<P>just finished all my front suspension work last week.  I didn't put lowering springs on though.  You will be glad you did when it comes time to reassemble.  The 3 inches would have saved me about two hour of wrestling with the stock springs to get them back in.<P>You probably will only have to loosen the rack and push it up to get the lower control arm bolt out.  It wasn't as bad as I expected.  I think the worst part of the whole thing was burning the bushings out.  What a mess!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000011||batboy||10-09-1999||09:29 PM||||Mshill, you were right about just needing to loosen up the steering rack a little to get those front bolts off the lower control arms. Burning out the old rubber bushings was not bad at all. Actually it worked quite well, just a little smelly.<P>Ok, an update and a couple lessons that I learned the hard way. I drove 20 miles into town and got the old worn out lower ball joints pressed off and the new ones pressed on. After getting home, I had a terrible time getting the new rubber boots installed and finally ripped one. In the past I used a hunk of pipe the right diameter to seat the boots, but couldn't find one the right size this time, so I tried just pushing it down in place with a screwdriver. Of course I poked a hole into the boot. Lesson #1: be careful when installing the rubber boots. Don't use sharp objects.<P>I had to drive 20 miles back to town again, then was unable to find a replacement boot after visiting 5 auto parts stores. I finally had to buy another ball joint just for the boot (I might return it and claim it had missing parts). I also stopped at the hardware store and bought a small section of pipe to fit over the boot. Using the pipe and a couple small taps with a hammer, the boots went right on with no problem.<P>Lesson #2: with the new poly bushings in place, I installed the lower control arm and coil spring. Something didn't look right. I finally realized that the lower end of the coil spring is supposed to sit into a little pocket on the control arm. I had to take everything apart again and rotate the coil spring until everything lined up correctly. Same at the top, there is a pocket on the rubber cushion too. Duh!<P>When I was putting the shocks on, I noticed that one of the lower shock bolts was just a regular bolt, not a hardened bolt. Fortunately, I have a whole supply of grade 5 and grade 8 bolts, so I replaced it with a better one. My advise is to always use hardened bolts on suspension parts.<P>I was far from done, but it was getting dark, I was tired, and the skeeters were eating me up, so I quit for the night. Tomorrow I'm installing the upper control arms and sway bar, with new poly bushings too of course. I forgot to get tie rod ends both times I went to town, so that means I'll be making another road trip again. Fun, fun, fun!||||reg||1
Z||000012||lowCG||10-09-1999||10:01 PM||||Sounds like you're getting into the dark parts of the car,that are never seen,just heard from when things wear out.<BR>FYI,the forward control arm bolts will remove with the tie rod,and control arm are nearly parallel to ground(jackstand under control arm,lower jack),freaked out the first time I saw that too(how could they!). ||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||10-10-1999||08:51 PM||||I was putting the front end back together today and as I tightened down one of the sway bar bushing bolts, it broke. I spent the next hour drilling that sucker out. Of course all my drill bits were duller than Bob Dole.<P>Then I couldn't find a tap or another bolt the right size. Screw it, I went inside and watched the KC Chief's football game. (They opened up a can of whoop ass on New England, yea!) Afterwards, I drove 20 miles into town again and got a bolt and tap. Again, I forgot the tie rod ends.<P>I finished assembling the front end and just put the old tie rods back on finger tight to check out the stance. The car is just a tad over 1-1/2 inches lower now than before. I plan on getting new front tires that are an inch lower than what's on it now, that should drop the front another 1/2 inch for a total of 2 inches lower than before. A very noticeable difference.<P>LowCG, the lower control arms points up slightly on the wheel side, about a 1/4 inch or so and the sway bar points up about the same amount. Do you think this is too much, or can I live with this. I'll probably grind about a 1/4 inch off the sway bar end link tube to level the bar, but not much I can do with the control arm without changing the springs, which I don't really want to do.<P>I pushed down on the front of the Batmobile and then did the same to my GT (both are 85 models). The Batmobile felt twice as firm, I hope it's not to hard. It'll probably shake the filling out of my teeth if I ever drive it on a long trip.<P>Unfortunately, I'll be away from home for the next week, so I won't be able to finish the front end or drive it for a while. Stay tuned for the next update and I'll let you know how it all worked out.||||reg||1
Z||000014||SCCA FIERO||10-10-1999||11:08 PM||||My friend had an 86GT with all poly, stiffer lowering springs and rear swaybar. It rode very well. It didn't bounce but more of a rocking effect. That was actually kinda fun, but when you turned it, IT TURNED FLAT!! Couldn't feel any body roll.||||reg||1
Z||000015||lowCG||10-10-1999||11:19 PM||||Oh yeah,1/4" only is fine,actually the limit I've found,depending on the travel used,so you're probably in good shape,actually the front end should drop a little more as the springs/rubber settle over the next week or so.If you notice something doesn't feel right in the way it drives after it's aligned,you may need to put a small shim in there to raise it slightly,and correct the goemetry,but you'll know if that's needed after driving.Beware of the "seat of the pants" factor;it's going to feel better initially because of the firmness,but,if dips(which may regularily allow the suspension to go into bad geometry) make the car feel loose,or uncontrolled,then...<BR>||||reg||1
